be starting now  hello everyone my name is jonas korloff  i'm chief scientific officer of pacific  biosciences  and i'd like to thank the organizers for  giving me an opportunity to speak to you  today  with regard to crispr cast 9 related  topics  my talk has two parts first i'd like to  talk about  the importance the challenges and new  solutions to measuring outcomes of  crispr cas9 experiments  and in the second part of my talk i  would like to mention new methods that  can  leverage the specificity of the cas9  enzyme  for targeted sequencing of previously  very difficult regions  so for the first part as an introduction  i think you all know that crispr  experiments are very easy to carry out  and i've just  highlighted two examples here in the  popular press  gene editing is so easy to do that we  couldn't stop if we wanted to  and there are even these homebrew mail  order crisper kits and so forth so  allowing absolutely anyone to hack dna  and so that  that just is a and there are many  research papers obviously and and  commercial kits now that  you know highlight how simple and how  straightforward it is to do these types  of experiments and edit  the genome of different organisms  however  it needs to be recognized that it's much  much harder to measure  uh accurately and comprehensively what  actually happens when you do such an  experiment  and you know there's one desired outcome  which is the editing of the target  region but  because the crispr cast 9 enzyme system  introduces double strand breaks in the  dna it has been recognized that there  are  potential undesirable outcomes and they  are categorized  at occurring either at or near the  target region the region that  people are interested in editing and  i'll refer to those as on target regions  and then it has also been described that  undesirable events outcomes can happen  far away from the target region and i'll  refer those  to off target events and so this was  described a couple years ago  on the right you see a nice article  by the scientists new methods off-target  mutations  where the the article describes  bioinformatic methods software methods  to predict um uh similarities of the  guide rna and so forth and uh in vitro  and in vivo methods to try to measure  these off-target mutations  what i'd like to do in this talk is to  tell you about a few new methods  and papers that describe doing this  comprehensively across the entire genome  and doing it with new methods  that give a essentially a better um  magnifying glass as you see it here in  the picture on the right  to see um better what the outcomes of  these genome editing experiments  are so i'd like to just very briefly  touch on  four papers and with the limited time i  can't go  in detail it's basically meant to give  you a teaser and if you're interested  you can  read those papers in more detail where  this has been described so there are  a number of examples over the last  couple years  where this has been investigated and  specifically using  new sequencing techniques so this is a  paper a couple years ago  in nature biotechnology a repair of  double strand breaks induced by crispr  cast 9 leads to large deletions and  complex rearrangements  so this paper is looking at the on  target undesirable effects  and you can see on the right side here  in this graph  there are two stacked images the first  one on the left  is looking around the target region  which is donated by the black lines in  about five or six kilobase scale so this  is looking  much further around that region and the  stack on the right is looking at 100  base pairs and so  you can see that when you only look at  very closely  you know in at the target region it  might not look so bad um  and there may be a little bit of a  depression but things look reasonably  okay  however when you look at it further out  around this particular region you see  that there are pretty large depressions  of reed coverage  and breaks in the read coverage and so  on the left side on the bottom left are  just a couple of the  undesirable effects that these  researchers recognized  where portions from a completely  different gene  in the top were introduced at the break  site  and then the second and third are  inversions either small or large  that were introduced by the crispr cast  nine  and so looking at this region with  longer sequence reads  um and sequencing that full length gives  you a better magnifying glass of what  actually happened  and so um just one more figure from this  paper  um you know if everything worked well  you would just see a dot  and the lines uh and the colorings  indicate that  there's a quite a variety of different  effects that can happen  both deletions uh in black and  insertions in orange  and so these can now be measured and  quantified  and then obviously that is a foundation  to then  adjust the experimental parameters to  minimize or  fix and avoid altogether these types of  undesirable effects  similarly a newer paper from this year  i'm from a chinese research group  also looked at cas9 cleavage  products and also find large fragment  deletions very similar  with these types of plots again the  coverage plot in orange on the right  uh you have the extend of the deletions  and you see again on the scale bar in  the bottom right  uh six kilobases so you really need to  look at it  at the kilobase scale which now can be  done um for example with pec bio  long read hifi sequencing to really  comprehensively and accurately identify  what happened at the site  that was targeted so again this is an on  target  method this was a much earlier paper  really pioneering um  in metfordius lab from stanford and nail  hindu  in cell reports uh characterizing and  describing smart sequencing in  particular  as a great way to quantify genome  editing outcomes  um they describe the system uh to work  on for all the common nucleus platforms  so not just crispr cast 9 but also zinc  fingers and talons and so forth  it can detect large insertion deletion  gene editing events  because of the long read lengths from  smart sequencing  it's broadly applicable to any cell type  and adaptable to any target site  and again it allows them for  optimization of these parameters to  minimize and it's highly accurate fast  and expensive  on the right what you see in the cyan  color  are the different off-target effects and  again these can be fairly large  pieces of dna that are being inserted  during the repair  process of the double strand break  and then the last paper that i would  like to mention and this is a new paper  the preprint  uh by eda hoyer and uh adam amir is the  senior author and i believe  adam will adam is speaking at this very  conference so i  would imagine that he's going to discuss  this paper in much more detail  but in this paper um looking again at  unforeseen crispr cast 9 off target  activity  they not only look at the genome y by  sequencing the entire genome  in a reference free unbiased fashion but  also describe that the difference  in the sequence of the two different  alleles the maternal allele and the  paternal allele  have to be taken into account when you  want to look at  off-target activities because the  similarity of the sequence  relative to the guide rna will be  different in some cases depending on  whether you're looking at  the maternal allele and the paternal  allele and they they show this figure  shows  that you can have very different um  activities  on the two different alleles so  more from adam i would imagine in this  conference and it's a really  uh really terrific preprint that i uh  and very recent work that i would  encourage you to  take a look at so in summary for the  first part of this talk  um it is important to measure the  outcomes  of the genome editing comprehensively  both with regard to on-target  and off-target effects because for  in efficient and safe procedures  you know you need to minimize and  eliminate those types of effects  and there are new uh solutions available  based on long read sequencing  that can measure these types of outcomes  at the multi-kilo based scale around the  target size  or as i mentioned in the last example  for unbiased genome-wide sequencing  you avoid bias by the reference genome  you don't have to map reads anymore you  can do the novo assemblies  you avoid allelic effects and so forth  and so i think that's really the future  of looking at um these types of  experiments and the outcomes of  crispr catholic experiments in an  unbiased and comprehensive manner  and just for um you know one  related note this month's cover of the  journal genome research  is an artwork by aran reeve who's at the  nih a co-author on this paper that's  shown on the right  about using high fidelity long reads now  to  assemble and characterize the human  genome much more comprehensively than  before  even resolving centromeres and and so  forth and so this is now possible and it  is efficient and it's cost effective  to no longer rely on uh shortly  sequencing and mapping to the reference  genome  but you can get a much more  comprehensive view of the entire  in this particular case human genome or  your genome of interest  to make sure that you're not having  undesired off-target effects  um or on target effects from crispr cas9  genome editing experiments  so in the second part of my talk i'd  like to switch and talk about  opportunities that can  leverage the really tremendous  specificity of the cas9  enzyme for targeted sequencing and in  particular regions  that are very difficult previously to  get any other way  and these are medically relevant for  example  in the neurobiology space there are  over 40 known inherited neurological  disorders that are caused by the  so-called repeat expansions  so these are simple dna sequences that  can be  expanded in their repeat and repeated  over and over  from under 1000 bases up to hundreds of  kilobases  and the length of those repeated  expansions determine the phenotype and  whether you have a normal phenotype  versus a disease phenotype  and the problem is that these are for  example the figure here you have some  triplet repeats or penta nucleotide  repeats  that because they  have a very low complexity or have an  extreme sequence  composition they typically cannot be  amplified efficiently or at all  with pcr and uh so  you can't you know amplify them out um  from um  the genome the patient and the uh a  person you want to  test um because of their the nature of  these particular sequences and so you  have to find  alternative ways to try to target and  enrich for this region and the cas9  enzyme  provides really exciting opportunities  there so  the way this can be done with a cas9  based enrichment for targeted sequencing  is that you have your dna of interest on  the upper left and the first thing you  want to do is block the five and  five prime and three prime ends of the  extracted dna you can do that simply by  dephosphorylation  because there will be a ligation step in  the future and  you want to avoid ligating all the all  of the dna fragments  and so we're going to try to enrich for  the one  squiggle there in the middle that has  the black segment which is the target  region of interest  and so then you design guide rnas two  guide rnas  that flank this particular region and  again  as you'll see with in conjunction with  long-range sequencing  you can design something that has you  know let's say four or six  kilobases um around the target region  um so you only need two guide rnas and  that piece of dna will be  enriched and excised in its entirety and  so there's no  assembly or no tiling required and so  forth  and so then you're taking advantage of  the specificity of the guide rna  to then cut make two cuts and  make a piece of dna that incorporates  the  that contains the region of interest and  significantly  does also does not have any of the  blocked ends  so that means it is available for  ligation and so  in conjunction with for example smart  sequencing you can then ligate these  airplane adapters they can have barcode  barcodes which are here  designated in purple which i'll talk  about in a minute  that allows for a high level of  multiplexing  which makes it efficient and so you can  see that there was no pcr  in at all in this particular um  procedure  and that's why i'll refer to it as the  no amp or no amplification  method and now you have enriched for  just the dna that's ready for sequencing  that contains the region of interest  so there are a number of examples here  this is a busy table but basically  this method allows for base level  resolution of the expanded genomic  region  an accurate repeat count identification  of interruption sequences  i'll talk about that in a minute i  mentioned it's free of pcr  so you don't have any pcr bios or  copying errors it's looking at the  native dna  as it is um as it was extracted from the  cells  and you can look at somatic mosaicism so  um  and it's it's amenable to multiple  kilobases looking at  a long long expansion so i mentioned  barcodes um  and so here's just one example of an  experiment where you can  multiplex both several targets  as well as several samples so  in this particular example we mix  together  12 samples and eight targets so in the  figure on the right you see the  different targets  fmr1 for fraudulex syndrome http for  huntington  c9 or 72 for als uh ataxin 10  for an ataxia nhva and hbb for  thalassemia and the last two are sip  genes for pharmacogenetics  and all of those targets can be  interrogated in a single experiment at  the same time that's all one assay  and of course with the um selection of  the guide rnas you can customize  what what genes do you want to look at  for this particular sample and can have  really high flexibility in customizing  the targets that you would like to  interrogate and then  the colored bars represent the different  samples so these are control samples  that you see on the bottom n a with a  number from the thousand genomes project  etc  and there are 12 samples and all of them  again are pooled in a single reaction  which makes this method really high  throughput and cost efficient  and on the on the left there you see  some stats about how many  uh you know total reads and reads per  sample  reads per target um but on the y-axis  on the right graph you can see that all  of the bars  are well over 50 and 50 is even  overkill for generating a high consensus  on both alleles  so all of these will give high  confidence  calls for the repeat expansions we have  developed  uh in addition some useful visualization  tools  so you can hear count on the  and and see the expanded repeats so for  three different examples  for the huntington's the first two and  then fragile x  you see the normal allele and then the  expanded allele on the x-axis is the  repeat count  unit and this you see that the peak  peaks are broadening  for the expanded allele and this is well  known in terms of the biology  that because you have a sloppy  replication that in the organism  in the individual the exact number of  reads can vary and you get a  good sense of how broad that how how  broad that distribution is  another way to look at this is in what  we call the waterfall plots  um so here you see the two alleles and  the wreaths  belonging to each allele stacked up  you see with the colored  so the gray is cgg in this particular  case on the left which is the main  repeat but in orange you see agg and so  you can immediately see that the normal  allele had two  agg interruptions because it has two  orange lines  on the top left and then the expanded  allele has only one  and that's significant for the uh risk  assessment of  how likely it will be for a mother to  give birth to a son that would have the  uh disease and so forth and on the right  for huntington's  you see a more complex picture with the  main cag repeat  in gray and then a number of different  sequence  triplets um as interruptions and that  all can be quantified and seen really  clearly  and then you know the typical igb  visualization so at the sequence level  you see how clean the hi-fi reits are  and then you can zoom in  and look at the sequence context and see  really with high clarity you can see  there are certain deletions  that correspond to the different lengths  of the cag repeats  you can look at the specificity of this  of the guide rnas very easily  across the entire human genome so in  this particular graph  you see the the human genome you see the  numbers on top of the graph  the chromosomes one two three all the  way to x and y  and then the reads um because they're  kilobases long you can map them uniquely  where they came from in the human genome  and in this particular experiment  four targets where um uh  four loci were targeted uh huntington's  als  scott 10 and fm01 and you can see  there's very good read coverage  and then by the errors donate um two  instances  in the human genome where there was also  read coverage  meaning there was some enrichment and  you can then look at those and find that  this was um caused  by uh sequence similarity of those  particular regions on chromosome 2 and  chromosome 9  having some similarity with one of the  four guide rna pairs  and you can look at the  sequence at the base level  you can quantify the off target effect  based on the height of the peaks  and so it gives you a really good  comprehensive and unbiased  view of whether you need to optimize  things further  now for the targeted sequencing that's  fine because you can just  remove those but again coming back to  the first part for genome editing  experiments  you would look at that and say well  maybe i need to optimize  my protocols and to guide rnas further  in order to  avoid potential of target effects  so um just to summarize  this method the no i'm targeted  sequencing method  allows for high level multiplexing  currently up to 48 barcoded adapters for  sample multiplexing  and for targeted multiplexing we have 18  validated guide rnas  available but as i mentioned it's  expandable and customizable  to other regions of interest and you can  mix and match to  your area of research there are a number  of publications already available so i  just wanted to include that here  and we'll make the slides available um  so you can click and learn more  about what the research community has  already achieved with this method  and we certainly see a lot of  opportunities  for the future so the summary of this  part too is that  this amplification free targeted  enrichment  leveraging the crispr cas9 enzyme and  specificity  can be used to target hard to amplify  regions  you have no pcr bias or errors you can  multiplex targets and samples  in conjunction with smart sequencing you  span the full region of interest with  high base accuracy and uniform coverage  and you can characterize mosaicism and  for the particular example  not limited to that of course but just  as one example for repeat expansion  disorders  it allows for accurate repeat counting  for both alleles  identification of interruption sequences  and the characterization of mosaicism  so with that i hope i've given you an  overview of these two topics  and as always um i understand that  questions can be emailed which i'll be  happy to answer  and so again thank you for the  organizers and  thank you for your attention  okay great job uh well we're all set  pretty much  from here i'll just do some quick  editing on the front and back ends and  get your presentation ready for next  week  wonderful thanks so much again nice to  talk to you again  yeah you as well if anything comes up  feel free to reach out but if not  we will go from here okay great thanks  so much  have a good day thank you youtube bye  you

